<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghazi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ghazi is a title for a Muslim warrior fighting against non-Muslims, carrying religious prestige. Ghazi warriors attracted to the Byzantine frontier helped fuel the early expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>'s wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim</a>, was known as al-Ghazi.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The campaigns of Imam Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-<b>Ghazi</b> (1527–1543) brought great wealth through the systematic plunder of Ethiopian churches, monasteries and royal treasuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The test of survival came in 1529, when Imam Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-<b>Ghazi</b>, known as Ahmad Gran and armed with firearms obtained through Ottoman channels, inflicted a catastrophic defeat on Lebna Dengel at the Battle of Shimbra Kure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1146 he was assassinated by a slave, and his lands were divided: Mosul and Iraq to his eldest son Saif ad-Din <b>Ghazi</b> I, Aleppo and Edessa to his second son Nur ad-Din.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The forces of Imam Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-<b>Ghazi</b> of the Adal Sultanate fought the Ethiopians under Emperor Dawit II at Amba Sel, and Adal seized southern Ethiopia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-28">28 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The principality passed to al-Ashraf Musa and then to al-Muzaffar <b>Ghazi</b>, sons of al-Adil, and then to Ghazi's son al-Kamil Muhammad.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayyubid-emirate-of-mayyafariqin">Ayyubid Emirate of Mayyafariqin</a></li><li><blockquote>Faisal I died in 1933 and was followed by <b>Ghazi</b> and the boy king Faisal II, under the regent Abd al-Ilah.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-iraq">Kingdom of Iraq</a></li></ul></section>
